WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:01.968
-Thank you for coming back.

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.033
It's so great to see you
in person.

00:00:03.033 --> 00:00:04.367
Thank you for playing
"Cold As Ice."

00:00:04.367 --> 00:00:06.133
-Oh, thank you for --
Yeah, thank you for having me.

00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:07.200
As always, very excited.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.400
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.300
-We've never played
that game before.

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:10.434
-I know. I'm honored.

00:00:10.434 --> 00:00:11.567
-I don't know if we'll
ever play again.

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:14.467
-We'll see.
We'll see, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:15.934
-I have to talk to you
about this

00:00:15.934 --> 00:00:18.868
because you know I'm the biggest
fan of Weird Al Yankovic.

00:00:18.868 --> 00:00:20.133
-Yeah, yeah.
-He's my favorite.

00:00:20.133 --> 00:00:21.634
He was my first concert
I ever saw.

00:00:21.634 --> 00:00:22.667
-I think that's incredibly cool.

00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:24.267
-I love that guy so much.

00:00:24.267 --> 00:00:27.734
And you are playing Weird Al
in a biopic movie.

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:28.868
-Yes.

00:00:28.868 --> 00:00:30.000
-This is --
-It's awesome.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.567
-I mean, how --
I cannot wait for this.

00:00:32.567 --> 00:00:34.367
-It's the thing --

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:37.367
I'm rarely excited
to see the stuff that I'm in.

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:39.501
I'm very, very excited
to see that.

00:00:39.501 --> 00:00:42.400
It is -- I mean, you know,
it's a fully insane movie.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:44.701
It's one of the most fun things
I've ever done.

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:47.234
And yeah, I don't know
when exactly it'll be out --

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:48.534
hopefully later this year --

00:00:48.534 --> 00:00:50.367
but, you know,
it's one to look forward to.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:53.067
-How did it come about?
Have you ever met Weird Al?

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.200
-So, apparently --
'Cause when they --

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:56.767
When I talked to Al
for the first time, I was like,

00:00:56.767 --> 00:00:58.367
"I'm immensely flattered

00:00:58.367 --> 00:00:59.767
by the idea that you would
pick me to play you.

00:00:59.767 --> 00:01:01.367
But like, why me?"

00:01:01.367 --> 00:01:04.801
You know, I'm mystified,
but excited.

00:01:04.801 --> 00:01:08.400
And apparently, so --
like, 13, 14 years ago,

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.567
I sang a version
of a Tom Lehrer song

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:13.400
on "The Graham Norton Show"
in the UK.

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:15.033
-Sure.
-I sang "The Elements"

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:18.734
next to Colin Farrell
and a very bemused Rihanna.

00:01:18.734 --> 00:01:20.334
I think he was just like,
"Who is this kid,

00:01:20.334 --> 00:01:22.667
and why is he singing all of
'The Elements'?"

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:25.934
And I guess Al saw that

00:01:25.934 --> 00:01:27.834
and was like,
"This guy maybe gets it."

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:29.133
And so picked me to -- yeah.

00:01:29.133 --> 00:01:31.133
So I'm very, very lucky.
-I love that.

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.667
-And now I've gotten to --
you know, I got to have,

00:01:32.667 --> 00:01:35.100
like, accordion lessons with Al.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.467
So it's --
I can go to my grave --

00:01:36.467 --> 00:01:38.300
-I would start crying
thinking about it.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:41.133
You brought us an awesome photo
of you with Al's accordion.

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:44.434
-Yes. So I got lent
Al's accordion for the month.

00:01:44.434 --> 00:01:46.434
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:46.434 --> 00:01:47.601
-[ Laughs ]
-That is --

00:01:47.601 --> 00:01:50.501
That was my last day
with the accordion.

00:01:50.501 --> 00:01:52.300
I was very sad to be having
to say goodbye to it,

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:53.868
'cause he gave me
his own accordion

00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:56.033
to practice on for the month.

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:58.701
On day two of that,
I broke one of the straps.

00:01:58.701 --> 00:01:59.868
[ Audience groans ]
So immediately --

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:01.000
Yes, that's how I felt.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.367
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.367 --> 00:02:04.234
And then immediately
got them replaced and he was --

00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:06.100
Again, thankfully,
he's, like, the nicest guy.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.501
-Yeah.
-If he was furious about it,

00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:09.000
he kept it to himself.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.033
-I cannot wait
to see that movie.

00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.868
I'm so excited.

00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:13.968
I think the casting is perfect.

00:02:13.968 --> 00:02:16.000
I love that you said
yes to that.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.367
You obviously have
already been part

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:20.167
of one of the biggest
movie franchises in the world.

00:02:20.167 --> 00:02:23.834
And now fans of yours online
want you to play Wolverine.

00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:25.467
Have you heard this?
-I have heard this.

00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.067
This is something
that has, like, come up

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:28.868
every so often
for the last few years.

00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:31.067
And every time it comes up,
I'm like, "That's not true.

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:32.434
There's nothing behind that."

00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:34.267
And everyone's like,
"Oh, he said it might be true."

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:36.467
And I'm like, "No, I didn't.
I said the opposite of that."

00:02:36.467 --> 00:02:37.667
[ Laughter ]
-No, no, no.

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.501
You said the word "true."
That counts.

00:02:39.501 --> 00:02:41.067
-And then, like, every so often,

00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:42.901
I'll get bored answering
the question in a sensible way,

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:44.400
so I'll make a joke,
and every time I make a joke,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.701
I'm like, "Why did you do that?
That's gonna set --"

00:02:46.701 --> 00:02:49.200
So the other day, I was like,
"Prove me wrong, Marvel."

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:51.400
And then that has ignited
a whole thing.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.734
But yeah, I think it's 'cause
there -- like,

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:54.467
Wolverine in the comics
is fairly short,

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:55.767
so I think it's people
going like,

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:59.000
"Who's a short actor?
Him. He can maybe play him."

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.434
-That's why. Yeah.

00:03:00.434 --> 00:03:01.734
-Yeah, I think so.
-Well, yeah.

00:03:01.734 --> 00:03:03.934
I mean, I think you would be
a fantastic Wolverine.

00:03:03.934 --> 00:03:05.334
-That's very kind.
Thank you.

00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:07.734
I mean, anything that implies

00:03:07.734 --> 00:03:10.701
the fleetingest similarity
to Hugh Jackman

00:03:10.701 --> 00:03:11.934
is incredibly flattering.

00:03:11.934 --> 00:03:13.000
So I'm happy with that.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:14.834
-Yeah.
[ Audience cheers ]

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:16.634
I'm so happy you decided
to announce it on our show

00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:17.801
that you are playing Wolverine.

00:03:17.801 --> 00:03:19.000
This is great.
-No!

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.901
No, it's not.
-It's so exciting.

00:03:20.901 --> 00:03:23.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:23.167 --> 00:03:24.667
Come back -- [ Laughs ]

00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:25.734
Come back when
the movie comes out.

00:03:25.734 --> 00:03:26.834
-Yeah, okay.

00:03:26.834 --> 00:03:28.601
[ Laughter ]

00:03:28.601 --> 00:03:31.334
-We had Rupert Grint
on the show last week.

00:03:31.334 --> 00:03:33.434
-Cool.
-He's great. Funny.

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:36.567
We're talking -- And we talked
obviously about the reunion.

00:03:36.567 --> 00:03:38.701
-Yeah.
-The "Harry Potter" reunion.

00:03:38.701 --> 00:03:40.267
Was it 20 years?

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:42.868
-Yeah, it was 20 years
since the first film.

00:03:42.868 --> 00:03:46.033
-It's got to be --
It's got to be exciting

00:03:46.033 --> 00:03:48.501
or was it odd, is it emotional

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:51.033
when you meet your cast
that you grew up with basically?

00:03:51.033 --> 00:03:52.467
-It was all of that.

00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:53.934
I mean, it was really --
it was lovely.

00:03:53.934 --> 00:03:55.567
It was so nice to see --

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:57.567
some people obviously
you've kept in contact with,

00:03:57.567 --> 00:03:58.934
and some people
you haven't as much.

00:03:58.934 --> 00:04:00.834
It was lovely to see them again.

00:04:00.834 --> 00:04:04.567
People like the Phelps twins
who, you know, when we started,

00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:05.834
they were like 14 and I was 11.

00:04:05.834 --> 00:04:08.133
And at that age, that's,
like, a massive difference.

00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:09.501
-Yeah, they're like 80...
-Right.

00:04:09.501 --> 00:04:10.934
-...and you were 3.
-Right.

00:04:10.934 --> 00:04:13.501
That's how it felt at the time.
-"You're 14?! Oh, my God!"

00:04:13.501 --> 00:04:15.968
-And then now, it's just, like,
we're all men in our thirties.

00:04:15.968 --> 00:04:19.300
Like, it's all so flattened out
in a really nice way.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.400
So it was lovely seeing them.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.367
I was also -- I was reminded --
you know,

00:04:22.367 --> 00:04:23.634
I was kind of going back like,

00:04:23.634 --> 00:04:25.767
I'm very happy
to have these memories.

00:04:25.767 --> 00:04:27.000
It's amazing.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Also happy it's not
my day-to-day anymore.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:31.100
That is also, like --
it's kind of nice.

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:32.567
-Yeah, you made it through.

00:04:32.567 --> 00:04:33.934
-Yeah, yeah.
-And you turned out okay.

00:04:33.934 --> 00:04:35.501
-And it's nice to be able
to like, celebrate it.

00:04:35.501 --> 00:04:37.033
-Yeah, exactly.

00:04:37.033 --> 00:04:39.000
We were talking backstage,
and I said,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.934
do people just yell at you
all the time, like,

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:42.434
"Harry Potter!"
or whatever?

00:04:42.434 --> 00:04:45.133
And you said that people
yell, "Elijah Wood."

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:47.033
-I got that one quite recently.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.033 --> 00:04:49.701
So, there's a long-running thing
with me and Elijah

00:04:49.701 --> 00:04:51.901
getting mistaken for each other.

00:04:51.901 --> 00:04:54.634
Originally, "Lord of the Rings"
and the first "Potter" film

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:58.000
came out around the same time,
and we are both, you know --

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.868
the idea of us is very similar.

00:04:59.868 --> 00:05:03.200
We are both, like, short actors
with blue eyes and very pale.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.534
-"The idea of us."

00:05:04.534 --> 00:05:06.701
-Yeah, like, we don't actually
look that much alike,

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:08.067
but close enough.

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:10.000
And recently,
a guy just was driving past

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:11.400
on the street in New York,

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:13.701
and I was just standing on
the corner and he just yelled,

00:05:13.701 --> 00:05:15.267
"Hey, Elijah! I love your work!"

00:05:15.267 --> 00:05:18.100
And I was just like, "Cool!
Thanks, man! Have a good day!"

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.734
And in my head,
the thought process was like,

00:05:19.734 --> 00:05:22.167
"Well, I don't want to ruin
his day."

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:23.534
Like,
he's had a nice interaction

00:05:23.534 --> 00:05:25.000
with Elijah Wood,
as far as he's concerned.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.000
Like, why spoil that?

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.467
I don't know if he's a fan
of me, so just give him that.

00:05:30.467 --> 00:05:32.834
-You know, we were looking
through old pictures.

00:05:32.834 --> 00:05:35.133
We were gonna show pictures of
you and Elijah.

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:38.734
And instead, I found something
that I think is even cooler.

00:05:38.734 --> 00:05:42.400
You signed a photo for someone,
an autograph,

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.601
and I think this is really
your handwriting,

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:45.734
but you can tell me.

00:05:45.734 --> 00:05:47.734
And this is so kind of cute.

00:05:47.734 --> 00:05:50.400
-Yeah.
-Is this your signature?

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.267
-This is how I signed autographs

00:05:52.267 --> 00:05:54.868
from between the ages
of, like, 9 and 10.

00:05:54.868 --> 00:05:56.701
-"Magic..."
-"Magic rules OK"

00:05:56.701 --> 00:05:59.133
with a lightning-bolt
exclamation mark.

00:05:59.133 --> 00:06:01.934
And it was either you got that
or you got "Believe in magic"

00:06:01.934 --> 00:06:07.000
or you got "Magic works"
from, like, 2000 to 2008 maybe?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:07.934
-You don't do it anymore?

00:06:07.934 --> 00:06:09.167
-You know, I don't do it.

00:06:09.167 --> 00:06:11.367
It's less relevant to, like,
"Equus" and stuff.

00:06:11.367 --> 00:06:14.200
Like, it became "This is weird
to write this now."

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:15.434
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:15.434 --> 00:06:16.968
-But, yeah, that is
my terrible handwriting.

00:06:16.968 --> 00:06:19.667
What I'm confused about in this
is why I have apparently signed

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:24.167
a photo of myself with no one
else in it while I am on holiday

00:06:24.167 --> 00:06:26.067
on the Sydney Harbor Bridge.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:26.067 --> 00:06:27.434
-I don't know who wanted that,
was like,

00:06:27.434 --> 00:06:28.400
"Can I have an autograph?"
"Yes."

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.667
"But can I have it of you

00:06:29.667 --> 00:06:31.033
in front of the Sydney Opera
House on a bridge?"

00:06:31.033 --> 00:06:32.501
-In Australia, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:32.501 --> 00:06:34.300
-Well, it's
a collector's item now.

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:35.234
-Yeah, apparently.

00:06:35.234 --> 00:06:36.400
-'Cause you can't
get this anymore.

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:38.767
I want to talk about
"The Lost City."

00:06:38.767 --> 00:06:40.234
-Yeah.
-I'm excited about this movie.

00:06:40.234 --> 00:06:42.434
Sandra Bullock,
Channing Tatum --

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:43.801
both awesome people.

00:06:43.801 --> 00:06:45.033
-Yes, they really are.

00:06:45.033 --> 00:06:48.434
-Did you call Sandra Bullock
"Sandy?"

00:06:48.434 --> 00:06:50.534
-It took me so long to get to
that point with her

00:06:50.534 --> 00:06:52.100
where I's just be like --
-I'm not there.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.400
-I'm not -- I didn't feel like
I was there even after months

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.667
and she was like,
"Really, call me Sandy.

00:06:55.667 --> 00:06:57.367
You don't have to call me
Sandra."

00:06:57.367 --> 00:06:59.834
And then I would say
in conversation with my friends,

00:06:59.834 --> 00:07:00.901
I'd be telling a story
about work,

00:07:00.901 --> 00:07:02.100
and I'd be like,
"And then Sandy said --"

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:03.734
And they're like, "Oh, Sandy."

00:07:03.734 --> 00:07:05.934
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, Mr. Big shot.

00:07:05.934 --> 00:07:08.000
-Right.
It's what she prefers!

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.467
I hate informality, as well!

00:07:10.467 --> 00:07:11.868
[ Laughter ]

00:07:11.868 --> 00:07:14.133
-I would even call her
Sandra Bullock.

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:16.701
-I mean, yeah.
-I would say her full name.

00:07:16.701 --> 00:07:18.534
-It's definitely hard
to, like --

00:07:18.534 --> 00:07:20.501
Growing up on "Potter,"
I obviously worked

00:07:20.501 --> 00:07:21.634
with a lot of amazing actors.

00:07:21.634 --> 00:07:23.067
But I wasn't necessarily
familiar

00:07:23.067 --> 00:07:26.601
with the filmography of
Maggie Smith or Richard Harris

00:07:26.601 --> 00:07:30.667
in the way that I grew up
watching...Sandy's films.

00:07:30.667 --> 00:07:32.767
[ Laughter ]
And it was incredibly surreal.

00:07:32.767 --> 00:07:34.033
-That's how you say
her name now?

00:07:34.033 --> 00:07:35.834
Saaandy?
-"Saaandy."

00:07:35.834 --> 00:07:39.067
-"Hi, Channing.
Hello, Saaandy."

00:07:39.067 --> 00:07:40.934
[ Laughter ]

00:07:40.934 --> 00:07:42.067
But yeah.

00:07:42.067 --> 00:07:45.033
-Yeah, no.
-One of the nicest human beings.

00:07:45.033 --> 00:07:47.634
-And yeah, she's truly amazing.

00:07:47.634 --> 00:07:49.634
-How do we set up the film?

00:07:49.634 --> 00:07:55.334
-So, it is kind of old fashioned
adventure, rom-com movie.

00:07:55.334 --> 00:07:59.367
Sandra and Channing
play love interests.

00:07:59.367 --> 00:08:01.133
She is a romance novelist.

00:08:01.133 --> 00:08:02.534
Channing is her cover model.

00:08:02.534 --> 00:08:06.567
I am a crazy billionaire
who decides to kidnap Sandra

00:08:06.567 --> 00:08:10.000
because I think she has the key
to finding some treasure.

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:12.067
And it's a great, fun movie.

00:08:12.067 --> 00:08:13.534
I feel like
I've done it no justice.

00:08:13.534 --> 00:08:15.868
-No, that's exactly right.
Perfectly done.

00:08:15.868 --> 00:08:17.000
Perfectly said.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.234
It's a great popcorn movie.
I love it.

00:08:18.234 --> 00:08:19.567
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:21.267
Here's Daniel Radcliffe
and Sandra Bullock

00:08:21.267 --> 00:08:22.534
in "The Lost City."

00:08:22.534 --> 00:08:24.200
Take a look at this.

00:08:24.200 --> 00:08:30.501
♪♪

00:08:30.501 --> 00:08:31.501
-Hello.

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:37.601
-Where am I?

00:08:37.601 --> 00:08:39.234
-Um, you're on my plane.

00:08:39.234 --> 00:08:40.868
It's nice, isn't it?

00:08:40.868 --> 00:08:42.868
The seats are made
of mama llama leather.

00:08:42.868 --> 00:08:46.000
-Well,
I'm getting off your plane.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:47.634
Unchain me.

00:08:47.634 --> 00:08:49.334
-That's your seat belt.

00:08:49.334 --> 00:08:51.667
-Did you chloroform me?

00:08:51.667 --> 00:08:53.834
People actually do that?

00:08:53.834 --> 00:08:55.033
Oh.

00:08:55.033 --> 00:08:56.534
Oh!
-Oh.

00:08:56.534 --> 00:08:58.934
Well, you know,
it's a classic for a reason.

